<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand II, Archduke of Austria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1529">1529</a>–<a href="/years/1595">1595</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Ferdinand of Tyrol · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand II, Archduke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, was a son of Emperor Ferdinand I who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and Further Austria from 1564. In 1557 he secretly married the commoner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-welser">Philippine Welser</a>, so their sons were excluded from the succession; one of them, Charles, became Margrave of Burgau. A great collector, he assembled famous collections of art, armour and curiosities at Ambras Castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-archduke-of-austria.jpg" alt="Ferdinand II, Archduke of Austria" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Terzi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archduke_Ferdinand_II_of_Further_Austria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ferdinand was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand I, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-bohemia-and-hungary">Anna of Bohemia and Hungary</a>, and a younger brother of Emperor Maximilian II. He grew up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, where his father governed the Austrian hereditary lands on behalf of Ferdinand's uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. He was said to be his father's favourite son, and a visiting dignitary described him as handsome and friendlier than Maximilian. Among his teachers were Kaspar Ursinus Velius and Georg Tannstätter, and other young nobles were invited to court to be educated beside the two archdukes, speaking only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, Czech or other foreign languages with them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At his father's behest Ferdinand took charge of the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1547, and he led the campaign against the Turks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> in 1556. In 1557 he secretly married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-welser">Philippine Welser</a>, daughter of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> patrician. Emperor Ferdinand I accepted the marriage only in 1559, on condition of secrecy. Their children were to bear the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> but could inherit only if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a> became extinct in the male line, and their sons took the title Margrave of Burgau.</p><p>After his father's death in 1564 Ferdinand ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and other Further Austrian possessions under the will, but stayed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> until 1567 at his brother Maximilian's wish. In his own lands he made sure that the Catholic Counter-Reformation prevailed, and he promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. His collections of art, armour and curiosities were housed at Ambras Castle in a museum built for the purpose. They began in Bohemia, and were so costly that he fell deeply into debt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Philippine died in 1580, and in 1582 Ferdinand married his niece Anna Caterina Gonzaga, a daughter of William I, Duke of Mantua. He died on 24 January 1595. Because his sons by his first marriage could not inherit and the second marriage produced only daughters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> was reunited with the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands. His daughter Anna became empress as consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-holy-roman-emperor">Matthias, Holy Roman Emperor</a>. The Chamber of Art and Curiosities at Ambras is the only Renaissance Kunstkammer of its kind preserved at its original location, and part of the collections went to the Kunsthistorisches Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ferdinand II of Austria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1609 Günzburg was the residence of Margrave Charles of Burgau, son of <b>Archduke Ferdinand II</b> and Philippine Welser.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-burgau">Margraviate of Burgau</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Linz, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Schloss Ruhelust, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.31,14.29&amp;year=1571" aria-label="Linz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4882 474 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ferdinand II, Archduke of Austria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5332 744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5332 744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.31,14.29&amp;year=1571">What was here in 1571? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
